      <p style="margin-bottom:20px;">Sheffield sits directly on the Tennessee River's south bank, between the Wilson Dam reservoir and the Muscle Shoals area. This riverfront position creates the same high-humidity microclimate that affects all of the Quad Cities — summer humidity regularly above 85%, year-round moisture in the air that finds its way into crawlspaces, under sinks, and behind the walls of Sheffield's aging housing stock. Mold in Sheffield's mid-century homes is not rare — it's endemic in homes that haven't had professional moisture assessment and crawlspace inspection in the past five years.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom:20px;">Sheffield's low-lying terrain along the river creates flooding risk that extends beyond the riverfront itself. The storm drainage infrastructure in Sheffield's older neighborhoods was designed for routine rainfall — not the 3–5 inch events that the Tennessee Valley receives multiple times per year. When those events hit, water backs up in basements, at the bases of foundations, and through crawlspace vents in ways that cause real structural damage. We've responded to flood events in Sheffield homes from multiple rain events over three decades.</p>
      <p>The Shoals' industrial environment — aluminum smelting, chemical processing, and manufacturing operations that have operated in this river corridor for generations — has historically contributed to particulate matter in the Sheffield air. Combined with North Alabama's high natural pollen loads, Sheffield homes accumulate allergen concentrations in carpet and upholstery that make professional cleaning as much a health issue as a cosmetic one.</p>
    `,
  }}
